class Solution:
    def maxProduct(self, nums: List[int], k: int, limit: int) -> int:


=== MODEL ===
This one is a DP over states (index parity, alternating sum, product). Since nums[i] ≤ 12 and limit ≤ 5000, products are bounded by limit (values of 0 need special handling, and products of 1s don't grow). I'll track, for each (parity of next position, current alt sum), the set of achievable products ≤ limit. Zeros make the product 0, which I handle by allowing product 0 as a state.
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "Q-5583", "code": "from typing import List\n\nclass Solution:\n    def maxProduct(self, nums: List[int], k: int, limit: int) -> int:\n        # State: (parity, alt_sum) -> set of achievable products (<= limit).\n        # parity = number of picked elements mod 2 (0 means next pick is added with +,\n        # 1 means next pick is subtracted with -).\n        # alt_sum is bounded by |sum| <= 150*12 = 1800 in magnitude.\n        OFF = 2000\n        # dp maps (parity, sum) -> set of products\n        dp = {}\n        for x in nums:\n            new_items = []\n            # Starting a new subsequence with x (it's the first, so +x, parity becomes 1)\n            if x <= limit:\n                new_items.append(((1, x), x))\n            for (par, s), prods in list(dp.items()):\n                if par == 0:\n                    ns = s + x\n                else:\n                    ns = s - x\n                npar = 1 - par\n                for p in prods:\n                    np_ = p * x\n                    if np_ <= limit:\n                        new_items.append(((npar, ns), np_))\n            for key, prod in new_items:\n                dp.setdefault(key, set()).add(prod)\n        best = -1\n        for (par, s), prods in dp.items():\n            if s == k:\n                for p in prods:\n                    if p > best:\n                        best = p\n        return best\n"}

