I'll use digit DP. State: position, sum of digits so far (max 81), product of digits so far (product is 0 or a product of digits 1–9, which are of the form 2^a 3^b 5^c 7^d; I'll track the product as a value in a dictionary), tight flag, and started flag. Count numbers ≤ X that are beautiful, then answer = f(r) − f(l−1).

Note that a number with a zero digit has product 0, which is divisible by any positive sum, so it is beautiful.
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "Q-6001", "code": "from functools import lru_cache\n\n\nclass Solution:\n    def beautifulNumbers(self, l: int, r: int) -> int:\n        def count_upto(x: int) -> int:\n            # Count beautiful positive integers in [1, x]\n            if x <= 0:\n                return 0\n            digits = list(map(int, str(x)))\n            n = len(digits)\n\n            # Distinct product values reachable: product of digits 1..9 (and 0)\n            # We memoize on (pos, digit_sum, product, tight, started)\n            @lru_cache(maxsize=None)\n            def dp(pos, dsum, prod, tight, started):\n                if pos == n:\n                    if not started:\n                        return 0\n                    return 1 if prod % dsum == 0 else 0\n                limit = digits[pos] if tight else 9\n                total = 0\n                for d in range(limit + 1):\n                    nt = tight and d == limit\n                    if not started and d == 0:\n                        total += dp(pos + 1, 0, 1, nt, False)\n                    else:\n                        nprod = 0 if d == 0 else (prod * d if started else d)\n                        if started and prod == 0:\n                            nprod = 0\n                        total += dp(pos + 1, dsum + d, nprod, nt, True)\n                return total\n\n            result = dp(0, 0, 1, True, False)\n            dp.cache_clear()\n            return result\n\n        return count_upto(r) - count_upto(l - 1)\n"}

