class Solution:
    def maxProduct(self, nums: List[int], k: int, limit: int) -> int:


=== MODEL ===
Approach: DP over elements with state (alternating sum so far, parity of subsequence length, product). Since nums[i] ≤ 12 and limit ≤ 5000, the product can be tracked as an exact value ≤ limit (products with a 0 become 0, handled separately). The alternating sum is bounded by roughly ±150·12 = ±1800, so it's a manageable range.

State: reachable[(sum, parity)] = set of achievable products ≤ limit. Position parity determines sign: if the next element is at an even index in the subsequence (parity 0), it's added; at odd index, subtracted. Products > limit are discarded, and any product including a 0 is 0 (kept as a value).

I'll implement this with a dictionary mapping (sum, parity) to a set of products, iterating through nums and extending each state with the current element (either skipping or taking it).
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "Q-5583", "code": "from typing import List\n\n\nclass Solution:\n    def maxProduct(self, nums: List[int], k: int, limit: int) -> int:\n        # state: (alternating_sum, parity_of_length) -> set of achievable products <= limit\n        # parity_of_length = number of chosen elements mod 2 (0 means next chosen goes to even index -> add)\n        states = {}\n        for x in nums:\n            new_states = {key: set(vals) for key, vals in states.items()}\n            # Start a new subsequence with x as first element (index 0 -> add)\n            if x <= limit:\n                new_states.setdefault((x, 1), set()).add(x)\n            for (s, p), prods in states.items():\n                if p == 0:\n                    ns = s + x  # next index is even -> add\n                else:\n                    ns = s - x  # next index is odd -> subtract\n                np_ = 1 - p\n                for pr in prods:\n                    np_prod = pr * x\n                    if np_prod <= limit:\n                        new_states.setdefault((ns, np_), set()).add(np_prod)\n            states = new_states\n\n        best = -1\n        for (s, p), prods in states.items():\n            if s == k and prods:\n                best = max(best, max(prods))\n        return best\n"}

