Approach: elements equal to k already count as a base frequency. For each target value v ≠ k, we choose a subarray where adding x = k - v maps v to k; the best subarray maximizes (count of v) minus (count of k) over the segment, computed with Kadane's algorithm per value. The answer is the base count of k plus the best such gain (never less than the base count).
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "Q-2863", "code": "from typing import List\n\nclass Solution:\n    def maxFrequency(self, nums: List[int], k: int) -> int:\n        base = nums.count(k)\n        best_gain = 0\n        for v in range(1, 51):\n            if v == k:\n                continue\n            # Kadane: +1 for each v, -1 for each k; max subarray sum\n            cur = 0\n            mx = 0\n            seen = False\n            for x in nums:\n                if x == v:\n                    seen = True\n                    cur = cur + 1 if cur > 0 else 1\n                elif x == k:\n                    cur = cur - 1\n                    if cur < 0:\n                        cur = 0\n                else:\n                    pass\n                if cur > mx:\n                    mx = cur\n            if mx > best_gain:\n                best_gain = mx\n        return base + best_gain\n"}

