Starter code:

class Solution:
    def solveQueries(self, nums: List[int], queries: List[int]) -> List[int]:


=== MODEL ===
Approach: group indices by value. For each value with positions p_0 < p_1 < ... < p_{c-1} (sorted), the circular distance from p_t to a neighbor is min(|p_t - p_s|, n - |p_t - p_s|). The nearest same-valued index is always one of the two circular neighbors in the sorted list (previous and next, wrapping around). So for each query, look up its position in its value's sorted list and check the two neighbors with circular distance. If the value has only one occurrence, answer is -1.

Checking example 1: nums = [1,3,1,4,1,3,2], n = 7. Value 1 at positions [0,2,4]. Query 0 (position 0): neighbors 2 (dist 2) and 4 (dist min(4,3)=3), so 2. Matches. Value 3 at [1,5]; query 5: neighbors 1 with dist min(4,3)=3. Matches.
TOOL submit_solution {"ticket_id": "Q-3523", "code": "from typing import List\nfrom collections import defaultdict\nimport bisect\n\nclass Solution:\n    def solveQueries(self, nums: List[int], queries: List[int]) -> List[int]:\n        n = len(nums)\n        pos = defaultdict(list)\n        for i, v in enumerate(nums):\n            pos[v].append(i)\n        res = []\n        for q in queries:\n            plist = pos[nums[q]]\n            c = len(plist)\n            if c == 1:\n                res.append(-1)\n                continue\n            idx = bisect.bisect_left(plist, q)\n            best = n\n            # previous neighbor (circular)\n            prev_i = plist[(idx - 1) % c]\n            d = abs(q - prev_i)\n            d = min(d, n - d)\n            best = min(best, d)\n            # next neighbor (circular)\n            next_i = plist[(idx + 1) % c]\n            d = abs(q - next_i)\n            d = min(d, n - d)\n            best = min(best, d)\n            res.append(best)\n        return res\n"}

