This is the classic "Divide the potions" problem (LeetCode 3494 style). The standard solution: for consecutive potions j-1 and j, the start time of potion j is determined by the maximum over wizards of the constraint that potion j must not start before potion j-1 finishes on each wizard. Define for each adjacent pair of potions the gap:

gap = max over i of (prefix_skill[i+1] * mana[j-1] - prefix_skill[i] * mana[j])... more precisely, with S_i = sum of skill[0..i], the required start offset between consecutive potions is max over i of (S_i * mana[j-1] - S_{i-1} * mana[j]), where S_{-1} = 0. Equivalently, for each wizard i, the time of potion j-1 ending at wizard i relative to the start, minus the time potion j starts at wizard i, and the total answer is the sum of these offsets plus the last potion's total time through all wizards: sum_j offset_j + mana[last] * S_{n-1}.

