=== MODEL THINKING (summary) ===
I'm trying to recall which LeetCode problem this matches, and I think the known approach is to count the values equal to k outside the chosen subarray and add the best count achievable inside it. Inside the subarray, each element can be shifted to k by picking a shift that matches one of its values, so the question becomes which shift covers the most elements.

I'm realizing the chosen shift x is fixed, so the elements that become k are those equal to k minus x. Picking x as k minus some value v means the count is the k's outside the subarray plus the v's inside it, which reduces to maximizing a Kadane-style subarray sum per v. I'm checking this against the first two examples and it matches.

